# Language split — you (agent) vs customer

## Hard rule

| Audience | Language |
|----------|----------|
| **You (the operator / chat user)** | **English only** — all agent-facing chat prose |
| **Customer** (Sprinklr email body / section 6 / PR paste) | **German only** |

## Agent-facing (English)

Use **English** for:

- RE sections **1–5** and **7** (summary, verification, transfer, handling, English recap)
- Warnings (`PAUSE_AUTO_LF`, Speichern gate, wrong chat, model errors)
- Armed / status quotes (`PR done for #…`, `LF TR done`, `Armed — click …`)
- CALL handling packs (optional BRIEF coaching)
- Instructions-dashboard replies
- Explanations, questions, and confirmations to **you**

**Do not** write agent guidance, section headers’ body text, or status messages to you in German.

## Customer-facing (German)

Use **German only** in:

- RE **section 6** (customer reply code block)
- **PR** paste / `temp_reply.txt` / Sprinklr editor
- Fixed salutation, body, survey, signature per `reply-salutation.mdc`

Section 6 and PR must follow all existing German template rules (umlauts, no internal systems, etc.).

## Allowed German inside English sections

- **Quoting** the customer’s email subject/body when needed for context (short excerpts)
- **Section 6** code block (that block is 100% German)
- Proper nouns / product names (**Mein o2**, **o2.de**) inside English handling text

## Forbidden

- German prose explaining the case **to you** in sections 1–5 or 7
- English text inside the customer reply (section 6 / PR)
- Mixing languages in one agent-to-you sentence unless quoting the customer

## RE quick map

```
1–5, 7  → English (to you)
6       → German (to customer, code block)
PR      → German (mail format from section 6)
```
